<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The launcher module's manifest, overridden for exactly one reason: to turn Android's
    Auto Backup off.

    Why it has to be here and not in a library. `GlimmerBackup.androidlib` declared the
    same attribute first and it did not work, and the way it failed is the point: the
    AppsFlyer SDK's own manifest sets `android:allowBackup="true"`, two libraries at the
    same level cannot resolve a conflict between themselves, and `tools:replace` in one
    of them does not settle it. The first attempt failed the build outright; the second
    merged green and silently kept `true`. Only the application module outranks every
    library, and that is this file. Proved by running the manifest merger and reading
    `allowBackup` out of the merged result, because it is the one fault here that leaves
    no trace: the build succeeds and the setting is simply not applied.

    Why Auto Backup is refused at all. It is on by default and copies `shared_prefs`,
    `databases` and `files` off the device, then restores them onto a reinstall or a new
    phone. For an app whose cross device story is an account, that is a second sync path
    nobody designed, and it cannot merge: it is a blind file restore, where every join
    this game makes is idempotent and monotonic (invariant 11). Three things it restores
    are actively poisonous, and all three were found on a real device.

    `shared_prefs/com.google.firebase.auth.api.Store.*` is Firebase's persisted session,
    encrypted against `com.google.firebase.auth.api.crypto.*`, which is regenerated per
    install. Restored, it cannot be decrypted, so the SDK holds no user while everything
    derived from it still names the old account. `databases/firestore.*` is the Firestore
    offline cache; restored, it serves another install's documents to a client that
    cannot authenticate, so the game draws a name, a wallet and a roster with no session
    behind any of it. And the Unity player preferences carry two things the invariants
    already refuse travelling: the cached update wall, which is a fact about this install
    on this platform (invariant 49c), and the remembered chapter per mode, which is
    device local and never in the save (invariant 8b).

    What it costs, stated rather than discovered: a player who reinstalls without signing
    in loses a grove that was only ever on that phone. That is what the account panel
    already tells them, and what an anonymous account costs regardless, since its uid
    dies with the installation.

    What this file freezes. Everything else here is Unity's own generated launcher
    manifest, copied verbatim, so `installLocation`, the icon references, the label and
    the screen support no longer follow Player Settings. They are four attributes and
    they are listed here so a change to any of them is a change to this file too.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
          android:installLocation="preferExternal">

    <application android:icon="@mipmap/app_icon"
                 android:label="@string/app_name"
                 android:roundIcon="@mipmap/app_icon_round"
                 android:allowBackup="false"
                 android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/glimmer_data_extraction_rules"
                 tools:replace="android:allowBackup,android:dataExtractionRules" />

    <supports-screens android:smallScreens="true"
                      android:normalScreens="true"
                      android:largeScreens="true"
                      android:xlargeScreens="true"
                      android:anyDensity="true" />
</manifest>
